Questions:
{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "Which of the following modeling statements is most appropriate?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "To compute the torque on a door about its hinges when you push near the handle, it is safe to model the door as a point mass located at its center.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This treats a rotational/rigid-body problem as pure translation. Torque depends on the lever arm and force application point, so a point-mass model throws away the geometry needed for rotation."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "To estimate how long a city bus takes to travel between two stops that are $8\\ \\mathrm{km}$ apart, it is usually reasonable to model the bus as a point mass when tracking its position along the route.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "When the distance scale of interest ($\\sim 8\\ \\mathrm{km}$) is much larger than the bus\u2019s size ($\\sim 10\\ \\mathrm{m}$), its internal dimensions are negligible for center-of-mass motion. In that regime, treating it as a point mass is a standard approximation for modeling translation (position vs. time) without resolving rotations or shape."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Even if you decide to ignore air resistance on a thrown ball, you must still include detailed airflow effects because the ball is not literally in a vacuum.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses a physical idealization with a literal claim about the environment. \u201cIgnore drag\u201d is equivalent to using a vacuum-force model for the motion equation."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above statements is appropriate.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option B is a valid scale-separation approximation for translational motion over kilometers. Therefore it is not correct that none are appropriate."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "Which of the following unit-conversion equations is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$1\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This applies the $10^{-3}$ factor for millimeters to area without squaring it. Area scales with the square of the length conversion."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$1\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Since $1\\ \\mathrm{mm}=10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m}$, squaring both sides gives $1\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}=(10^{-3}\\ \\mathrm{m})^2=10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$. The key idea is that powers apply to the conversion factor as well."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$1\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{6}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This inverts the metric prefix relationship. Millimeters are smaller than meters, so $\\mathrm{mm^2}$ must also be smaller than $\\mathrm{m^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$1\\ \\mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-2}\\ \\mathrm{m^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This treats \u201cmilli-\u201d like \u201ccenti-\u201d (a factor of $10^{-2}$) and also ignores squaring. The correct factor is $10^{-6}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "stem": "Which option has the incorrect SI unit?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Pressure: $\\mathrm{N/m^2}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Pressure is force per area, so $\\mathrm{Pa}=\\mathrm{N/m^2}$ is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "Angular momentum: $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Angular momentum has units $\\mathrm{(mass)(length^2)/(time)}$, so $\\mathrm{kg\\,m^2/s}$ is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Electric field: $\\mathrm{N/C}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Electric field is force per charge, so $\\mathrm{N/C}$ is correct."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Power: $\\mathrm{J\\cdot s}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Power is energy per unit time, so its SI unit is $\\mathrm{W}=\\mathrm{J/s}$. Writing $\\mathrm{J\\cdot s}$ multiplies by time instead of dividing, which corresponds to a different physical dimension."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "4",
      "stem": "A search-and-rescue drone\u2019s position relative to the command tent (at the origin) is $\\vec r = 4\\,\\hat\\imath + 11\\,\\hat\\jmath$ in kilometers. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The drone is $15\\ \\mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly adds components as $4+11$. The distance is the magnitude $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, not the Manhattan sum."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The drone is $11\\ \\mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the $y$-component with the total distance. The drone also has an $x$-offset."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The drone is $4\\ \\mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the $x$-component with the total distance. Both components contribute to the magnitude."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{4^2+11^2}=\\sqrt{137}\\approx 11.70\\ \\mathrm{km}$. Since $11.70$ km is not equal to $15$, $11$, or $4$, none of A\u2013C is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "5",
      "stem": "A cart\u2019s 1D displacement is given by $\\vec s(t)=\\big((1.10\\,\\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3-(2.40\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\\big)\\,\\hat\\imath$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This motion has constant acceleration.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "A $t^3$ term in $s(t)$ produces a time-dependent acceleration after two derivatives. Constant acceleration would require $s(t)$ to be at most quadratic in $t$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $4.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Compute velocity and acceleration: $v(t)=\\frac{ds}{dt}=3(1.10)t^2-2(2.40)t=3.30t^2-4.80t$ and $a(t)=\\frac{dv}{dt}=6.60t-4.80$. Thus $a(0)=-4.80\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$, so the magnitude is $|a(0)|=4.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is $2.4\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$ for all $t$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistakes a coefficient in $s(t)$ for the acceleration. Acceleration comes from $\\frac{d^2s}{dt^2}$, which here is $6.60t-4.80$, not a constant $2.4$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $0\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly assumes plugging in $t=0$ forces acceleration to vanish. Here $a(t)$ has a constant term, so $a(0)\\neq 0$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "6",
      "stem": "A robot moves along a straight path from position A, $\\vec r_A=1.5\\,\\hat\\imath-3.0\\,\\hat\\jmath$ (m), to position B, $\\vec r_B=-5.5\\,\\hat\\imath+4.5\\,\\hat\\jmath$ (m), taking $4.0\\ \\mathrm{s}$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance traveled is $14.5\\ \\mathrm{m}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This adds component changes as $|\\Delta x|+|\\Delta y|=7.0+7.5=14.5$, which is not the straight-line distance. For a straight path, the distance is the magnitude $\\sqrt{(\\Delta x)^2+(\\Delta y)^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at $t=0$ must be $2.6\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This treats the average speed as if it uniquely determines the initial speed. With only endpoints and total time, many velocity-time histories are possible."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance traveled is $10.3\\ \\mathrm{m}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Because the robot travels straight from A to B, the distance equals the displacement magnitude. $\\Delta\\vec r=( -5.5-1.5)\\hat\\imath+(4.5-(-3.0))\\hat\\jmath=-7.0\\hat\\imath+7.5\\hat\\jmath$, so $|\\Delta\\vec r|=\\sqrt{(-7.0)^2+(7.5)^2}=\\sqrt{105.25}\\approx 10.26\\ \\mathrm{m}\\approx 10.3\\ \\mathrm{m}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.64\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Acceleration cannot be determined from just start/end position and elapsed time. Infinitely many accelerations could connect the same endpoints in $4.0$ s."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "7",
      "stem": "A frog leaps from level ground with speed $v_0=7.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ at an angle $\\theta=40^\\circ$ above the horizontal (ignore air resistance). What is the frog\u2019s speed at maximum height?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$5.7\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "In ideal projectile motion, the horizontal component stays constant: $v_x=v_0\\cos\\theta$. At maximum height the vertical component is zero ($v_y=0$), so the speed is $v=\\sqrt{v_x^2+0^2}=v_0\\cos\\theta=7.5\\cos 40^\\circ\\approx 5.75\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$7.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This ignores that the vertical component decreases to zero at the top. Only the horizontal component remains."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$4.8\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This corresponds to $v_0\\sin\\theta$, the initial vertical component, not the speed at maximum height where $v_y=0$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "At the top only the vertical velocity is zero. The horizontal velocity generally remains nonzero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "8",
      "stem": "A glider on a frictionless air track has initial velocity $\\vec v_0=(12.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s})\\,\\hat\\imath$ at $t=0$. A fan provides a constant acceleration $\\vec a=(-1.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2})\\,\\hat\\imath+(2.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2})\\,\\hat\\jmath$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The $x$-component of the glider\u2019s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Because $a_x=-1.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\neq 0$, the $x$-component changes as $v_x(t)=v_{0x}+a_xt$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The $y$-component of the glider\u2019s velocity remains constant.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Because $a_y=2.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}\\neq 0$, the $y$-component changes from its initial value ($0$) to nonzero values."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The speed stays constant but the direction changes.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Constant speed would require acceleration always perpendicular to velocity, which is not the case here because $a_x\\neq 0$ affects the speed along the initial motion direction."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "Both the direction of motion and the speed change.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The nonzero $a_y$ creates a growing $y$-velocity component, so the velocity direction rotates away from the $+x$ axis. Also, since $a_x\\neq 0$, the component of acceleration parallel to the velocity is nonzero, so the speed (magnitude of $\\vec v$) changes with time."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "9",
      "stem": "Two point charges are separated by a distance $r$ and exert an electrostatic force of magnitude $F$ on each other. If each charge\u2019s magnitude is reduced to one-third of its original value and the separation is doubled (to $2r$), what happens to the force magnitude?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "It stays the same.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This would require the charge-product scaling to exactly match the $r^2$ scaling. Here both changes reduce the force, so it cannot remain unchanged."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "It decreases by a factor of $9$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This accounts for $q_1q_2\\to (1/3)(1/3)q_1q_2=\\frac{1}{9}q_1q_2$ but incorrectly ignores the inverse-square dependence on distance."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "It decreases by a factor of $36$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "By Coulomb\u2019s law, $F=k\\frac{|q_1q_2|}{r^2}$. The charge product scales by $(1/3)(1/3)=1/9$ and the squared distance scales by $(2r)^2=4r^2$, so $F' = F\\cdot \\frac{1/9}{4}=\\frac{F}{36}$; the force decreases by a factor of $36$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "It increases by a factor of $36$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This reverses the scaling in Coulomb\u2019s law. Reducing charge magnitudes and increasing separation both decrease the force rather than increase it."
        },
        {
          "key": "E",
          "text": "It decreases by a factor of $4$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This includes the distance effect ($1/(2^2)=1/4$) but ignores that both charges were also reduced, which introduces an additional $1/9$ factor."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "10",
      "stem": "Escape speed from a spherical world is $v_e=\\sqrt{\\frac{2GM}{R}}$. World Y has mass $M$ and radius $R$. World X has mass $0.40M$ and radius $0.20R$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "World X has a lower escape speed than World Y because it has smaller mass.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This considers only $M$ and ignores the dependence on $R$. Escape speed depends on the ratio $M/R$, not on $M$ alone."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "World X has the same escape speed as World Y because both $M$ and $R$ are smaller.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Having both parameters smaller does not guarantee cancellation. You must compare $\\frac{M}{R}$ for each world."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "World X has a higher escape speed than World Y (by a factor of about $1.41$).",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "From $v_e\\propto\\sqrt{M/R}$, the ratio is $\\frac{v_{e,X}}{v_{e,Y}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{0.40M/0.20R}{M/R}}=\\sqrt{2}\\approx 1.41$. Even though X is less massive, it is much more compact (smaller $R$), which increases $M/R$ and thus increases escape speed."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The escape speed depends on the spacecraft\u2019s mass, so it cannot be compared without that information.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "In Newtonian gravity (neglecting drag), $v_e=\\sqrt{2GM/R}$ is independent of the escaping object\u2019s mass. The spacecraft mass cancels when deriving the expression from energy conservation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "11",
      "stem": "A constant force $\\vec F=(-2.5\\ \\mathrm{N})\\,\\hat\\imath+(6.0\\ \\mathrm{N})\\,\\hat\\jmath$ acts on a particle as it moves in the plane from $\\vec r_i=(-1.2\\,\\hat\\imath+0.5\\,\\hat\\jmath)\\ \\mathrm{m}$ to $\\vec r_f=(3.8\\,\\hat\\imath+4.5\\,\\hat\\jmath)\\ \\mathrm{m}$. What is the work done by the force?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$11.5\\ \\mathrm{J}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Work is the dot product $W=\\vec F\\cdot\\Delta\\vec r=F_x\\Delta x+F_y\\Delta y$. Here $\\Delta\\vec r=(3.8-(-1.2))\\hat\\imath+(4.5-0.5)\\hat\\jmath=5.0\\hat\\imath+4.0\\hat\\jmath$, so $W=(-2.5)(5.0)+(6.0)(4.0)=-12.5+24=11.5\\ \\mathrm{J}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$41.6\\ \\mathrm{J}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from incorrectly using $W\\approx |\\vec F|\\,|\\Delta\\vec r|$, which only works when force is parallel to displacement. Here the force is not parallel to the displacement."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$24.0\\ \\mathrm{J}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only the $y$-component contribution $F_y\\Delta y$ and ignores the negative work from the $x$-component. Work adds contributions from all components via the dot product."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$-12.5\\ \\mathrm{J}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This uses only $F_x\\Delta x$ and ignores the positive work from the $y$-component. The total work is the sum $F_x\\Delta x+F_y\\Delta y$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "12",
      "stem": "A $3.2\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ cart moving to the right at $4.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ hits a bumper and rebounds to the left at $2.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$. The contact time is $0.15\\ \\mathrm{s}$. What is the magnitude of the average force on the cart during the collision?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$96\\ \\mathrm{N}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This can result from using $\\Delta v=4.5-2.0=2.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ (forgetting the velocity reverses sign). The correct $\\Delta v$ must use $v_f=-2.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$139\\ \\mathrm{N}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Use impulse-momentum: $F_{\\text{avg}}=\\frac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{m(v_f-v_i)}{\\Delta t}$. Taking right as positive, $v_i=+4.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ and $v_f=-2.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$, so $\\Delta v=-6.5\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ and $|F_{\\text{avg}}|=\\left|\\frac{3.2(-6.5)}{0.15}\\right|\\approx 1.39\\times 10^2\\ \\mathrm{N}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$31\\ \\mathrm{N}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is too small and typically comes from dividing by the wrong time interval or using only one speed instead of the change in velocity. Impulse depends on $v_f-v_i$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option B matches the impulse calculation (to rounding). Therefore it is not correct that none of the above is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "13",
      "stem": "A $72\\ \\mathrm{kg}$ snowboarder is initially at rest on nearly frictionless snow. A friend tosses a $250\\ \\mathrm{g}$ snowball directly toward the snowboarder at $90\\ \\mathrm{km/h}$, and the snowboarder catches it (the snowball sticks). What is the snowboarder\u2019s speed immediately after the catch?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$0.31\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This typically comes from forgetting to convert $90\\ \\mathrm{km/h}$ to $\\mathrm{m/s}$ (treating $90$ as $90\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$). Unit conversion is essential before applying momentum conservation."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$25\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly assumes the snowboarder ends up moving at (or near) the snowball\u2019s initial speed. In a perfectly inelastic catch with a much larger combined mass, the final speed must be much smaller."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$0.087\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Treat the catch as perfectly inelastic and conserve momentum: $m_b v_b=(m_s+m_b)v_f$. Convert $90\\ \\mathrm{km/h}$ to $v_b=25\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$ and use $m_b=0.250\\ \\mathrm{kg}$, $m_s=72\\ \\mathrm{kg}$: $v_f=\\frac{0.250\\cdot 25}{72.25}\\approx 0.0865\\ \\mathrm{m/s}\\approx 0.087\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$0.0087\\ \\mathrm{m/s}$",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This is a factor-of-10 error, often from a mistaken km/h conversion (e.g., dividing by $3.6$ twice) or misplacing a decimal in the final division."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Create a complete LaTeX document with:
1. A document preamble with necessary packages
2. Custom macros for question formatting (\QBlock, \Correct, \Wrong)
3. All questions formatted using the macros
4. Proper math formatting for all expressions

Set filename to "isomorphic_questions.tex"

output.latex_content:
% ===== Document Preamble =====
\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=0.5in]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage{enumitem}
\usepackage{needspace}
\usepackage{comment}

% ===== Colors =====
\definecolor{AnsRed}{RGB}{170,0,0}
\definecolor{WrongBlue}{RGB}{0,0,160}

% ===== Choice list styling (tight, consistent) =====
\setlist[enumerate]{%
  leftmargin=1.5em,
  itemsep=0.25em,
  topsep=0.25em,
  label=\textbf{\Alph*.}
}

% ===== Style Wrappers =====
\newcommand{\QuestionStyle}[1]{{\large\bfseries\boldmath #1}}
\newcommand{\AnswerStyle}[1]{{\large\bfseries\boldmath #1}}
\newcommand{\ReasonStyle}[1]{{\normalsize #1}}

% ===== Show / Hide Reasoning Toggle =====
\newif\ifshowreason
\showreasontrue      % Instructor version
% \showreasonfalse   % Student version

% ===== Choice Item Macros (Answer + Reasoning) =====
% Usage:
%   \Correct{Answer}{Reasoning}
%   \Wrong{Answer}{Reasoning}

\newcommand{\Correct}[2]{%
  \item \AnswerStyle{\textcolor{AnsRed}{#1}}%
  \ifshowreason\\[-0.2em]
    \ReasonStyle{\textcolor{AnsRed}{#2}}%
  \fi
}

\newcommand{\Wrong}[2]{%
  \item \AnswerStyle{\textcolor{WrongBlue}{#1}}%
  \ifshowreason\\[-0.2em]
    \ReasonStyle{\textcolor{WrongBlue}{#2}}%
  \fi
}

% ===== Question Block Macro =====
% Usage: \QBlock{Q1}{stem...}{<items>}

\newcommand{\QBlock}[3]{%
  \Needspace{8\baselineskip}%
  \noindent
  \begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
    \QuestionStyle{#1.~#2}

    \vspace{0.25em}
    \begin{enumerate}
      #3
    \end{enumerate}

    \vspace{0.25em}
    \hrule height 0.4pt
  \end{minipage}
  \vspace{0.75em}
}

% ===== Document Start =====

\begin{document}

\begin{center}
{\huge \textbf{Physics Isomorphic Questions}}\\[0.25em]
{\Large Modeling, Units, Vectors, and Mechanics}\\[0.25em]
{\large Multiple choice questions generated below. Answers in RED and incorrect answers in BLUE.}
\end{center}

\vspace{0.75em}

\QBlock{Q1}{Which of the following modeling statements is most appropriate?}{
  \Wrong{To compute the torque on a door about its hinges when you push near the handle, it is safe to model the door as a point mass located at its center.}{This treats a rotational/rigid-body problem as pure translation. Torque depends on the lever arm and force application point, so a point-mass model throws away the geometry needed for rotation.}
  \Correct{To estimate how long a city bus takes to travel between two stops that are $8\ \mathrm{km}$ apart, it is usually reasonable to model the bus as a point mass when tracking its position along the route.}{When the distance scale of interest ($\sim 8\ \mathrm{km}$) is much larger than the bus's size ($\sim 10\ \mathrm{m}$), its internal dimensions are negligible for center-of-mass motion. In that regime, treating it as a point mass is a standard approximation for modeling translation (position vs.\ time) without resolving rotations or shape.}
  \Wrong{Even if you decide to ignore air resistance on a thrown ball, you must still include detailed airflow effects because the ball is not literally in a vacuum.}{This confuses a physical idealization with a literal claim about the environment. ``Ignore drag'' is equivalent to using a vacuum-force model for the motion equation.}
  \Wrong{None of the above statements is appropriate.}{Option B is a valid scale-separation approximation for translational motion over kilometers. Therefore it is not correct that none are appropriate.}
}

\QBlock{Q2}{Which of the following unit-conversion equations is correct?}{
  \Wrong{$1\ \mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m^2}$}{This applies the $10^{-3}$ factor for millimeters to area without squaring it. Area scales with the square of the length conversion.}
  \Correct{$1\ \mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$}{Since $1\ \mathrm{mm}=10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m}$, squaring both sides gives $1\ \mathrm{mm^2}=(10^{-3}\ \mathrm{m})^2=10^{-6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$. The key idea is that powers apply to the conversion factor as well.}
  \Wrong{$1\ \mathrm{mm^2}=10^{6}\ \mathrm{m^2}$}{This inverts the metric prefix relationship. Millimeters are smaller than meters, so $\mathrm{mm^2}$ must also be smaller than $\mathrm{m^2}$.}
  \Wrong{$1\ \mathrm{mm^2}=10^{-2}\ \mathrm{m^2}$}{This treats ``milli-'' like ``centi-'' (a factor of $10^{-2}$) and also ignores squaring. The correct factor is $10^{-6}$.}
}

\QBlock{Q3}{Which option has the incorrect SI unit?}{
  \Wrong{Pressure: $\mathrm{N/m^2}$}{Pressure is force per area, so $\mathrm{Pa}=\mathrm{N/m^2}$ is correct.}
  \Wrong{Angular momentum: $\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$}{Angular momentum has units $\mathrm{(mass)(length^2)/(time)}$, so $\mathrm{kg\,m^2/s}$ is correct.}
  \Wrong{Electric field: $\mathrm{N/C}$}{Electric field is force per charge, so $\mathrm{N/C}$ is correct.}
  \Correct{Power: $\mathrm{J\cdot s}$}{Power is energy per unit time, so its SI unit is $\mathrm{W}=\mathrm{J/s}$. Writing $\mathrm{J\cdot s}$ multiplies by time instead of dividing, which corresponds to a different physical dimension.}
}

\QBlock{Q4}{A search-and-rescue drone's position relative to the command tent (at the origin) is $\vec r = 4\,\hat\imath + 11\,\hat\jmath$ in kilometers. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{The drone is $15\ \mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.}{This incorrectly adds components as $4+11$. The distance is the magnitude $|\vec r|=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$, not the Manhattan sum.}
  \Wrong{The drone is $11\ \mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.}{This confuses the $y$-component with the total distance. The drone also has an $x$-offset.}
  \Wrong{The drone is $4\ \mathrm{km}$ from the command tent.}{This confuses the $x$-component with the total distance. Both components contribute to the magnitude.}
  \Correct{None of the above is correct.}{The distance from the origin is $|\vec r|=\sqrt{4^2+11^2}=\sqrt{137}\approx 11.70\ \mathrm{km}$. Since $11.70$ km is not equal to $15$, $11$, or $4$, none of A--C is correct.}
}

\QBlock{Q5}{A cart's 1D displacement is given by $\vec s(t)=\big((1.10\,\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3-(2.40\,\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\big)\,\hat\imath$. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{This motion has constant acceleration.}{A $t^3$ term in $s(t)$ produces a time-dependent acceleration after two derivatives. Constant acceleration would require $s(t)$ to be at most quadratic in $t$.}
  \Correct{The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $4.8\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{Compute velocity and acceleration: $v(t)=\frac{ds}{dt}=3(1.10)t^2-2(2.40)t=3.30t^2-4.80t$ and $a(t)=\frac{dv}{dt}=6.60t-4.80$. Thus $a(0)=-4.80\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$, so the magnitude is $|a(0)|=4.8\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.}
  \Wrong{The magnitude of the acceleration is $2.4\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$ for all $t$.}{This mistakes a coefficient in $s(t)$ for the acceleration. Acceleration comes from $\frac{d^2s}{dt^2}$, which here is $6.60t-4.80$, not a constant $2.4$.}
  \Wrong{The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0$ is $0\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{This incorrectly assumes plugging in $t=0$ forces acceleration to vanish. Here $a(t)$ has a constant term, so $a(0)\neq 0$.}
}

\QBlock{Q6}{A robot moves along a straight path from position A, $\vec r_A=1.5\,\hat\imath-3.0\,\hat\jmath$ (m), to position B, $\vec r_B=-5.5\,\hat\imath+4.5\,\hat\jmath$ (m), taking $4.0\ \mathrm{s}$. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{The distance traveled is $14.5\ \mathrm{m}$.}{This adds component changes as $|\Delta x|+|\Delta y|=7.0+7.5=14.5$, which is not the straight-line distance. For a straight path, the distance is the magnitude $\sqrt{(\Delta x)^2+(\Delta y)^2}$.}
  \Wrong{The speed at $t=0$ must be $2.6\ \mathrm{m/s}$.}{This treats the average speed as if it uniquely determines the initial speed. With only endpoints and total time, many velocity-time histories are possible.}
  \Correct{The distance traveled is $10.3\ \mathrm{m}$.}{Because the robot travels straight from A to B, the distance equals the displacement magnitude. $\Delta\vec r=( -5.5-1.5)\hat\imath+(4.5-(-3.0))\hat\jmath=-7.0\hat\imath+7.5\hat\jmath$, so $|\Delta\vec r|=\sqrt{(-7.0)^2+(7.5)^2}=\sqrt{105.25}\approx 10.26\ \mathrm{m}\approx 10.3\ \mathrm{m}$.}
  \Wrong{The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.64\ \mathrm{m/s^2}$.}{Acceleration cannot be determined from just start/end position and elapsed time. Infinitely many accelerations could connect the same endpoints in $4.0$ s.}
}

\QBlock{Q7}{A frog leaps from level ground with speed $v_0=7.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$ at an angle $\theta=40^\circ$ above the horizontal (ignore air resistance). What is the frog's speed at maximum height?}{
  \Correct{$5.7\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{In ideal projectile motion, the horizontal component stays constant: $v_x=v_0\cos\theta$. At maximum height the vertical component is zero ($v_y=0$), so the speed is $v=\sqrt{v_x^2+0^2}=v_0\cos\theta=7.5\cos 40^\circ\approx 5.75\ \mathrm{m/s}$.}
  \Wrong{$7.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{This ignores that the vertical component decreases to zero at the top. Only the horizontal component remains.}
  \Wrong{$4.8\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{This corresponds to $v_0\sin\theta$, the initial vertical component, not the speed at maximum height where $v_y=0$.}
  \Wrong{$0\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{At the top only the vertical velocity is zero. The horizontal velocity generally remains nonzero.}
}

\QBlock{Q8}{A glider on a frictionless air track has initial velocity $\vec v_0=(12.0\ \mathrm{m/s})\,\hat\imath$ at $t=0$. A fan provides a constant acceleration $\vec a=(-1.5\ \mathrm{m/s^2})\,\hat\imath+(2.0\ \mathrm{m/s^2})\,\hat\jmath$. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{The $x$-component of the glider's velocity remains constant.}{Because $a_x=-1.5\ \mathrm{m/s^2}\neq 0$, the $x$-component changes as $v_x(t)=v_{0x}+a_xt$.}
  \Wrong{The $y$-component of the glider's velocity remains constant.}{Because $a_y=2.0\ \mathrm{m/s^2}\neq 0$, the $y$-component changes from its initial value ($0$) to nonzero values.}
  \Wrong{The speed stays constant but the direction changes.}{Constant speed would require acceleration always perpendicular to velocity, which is not the case here because $a_x\neq 0$ affects the speed along the initial motion direction.}
  \Correct{Both the direction of motion and the speed change.}{The nonzero $a_y$ creates a growing $y$-velocity component, so the velocity direction rotates away from the $+x$ axis. Also, since $a_x\neq 0$, the component of acceleration parallel to the velocity is nonzero, so the speed (magnitude of $\vec v$) changes with time.}
}

\QBlock{Q9}{Two point charges are separated by a distance $r$ and exert an electrostatic force of magnitude $F$ on each other. If each charge's magnitude is reduced to one-third of its original value and the separation is doubled (to $2r$), what happens to the force magnitude?}{
  \Wrong{It stays the same.}{This would require the charge-product scaling to exactly match the $r^2$ scaling. Here both changes reduce the force, so it cannot remain unchanged.}
  \Wrong{It decreases by a factor of $9$.}{This accounts for $q_1q_2\to (1/3)(1/3)q_1q_2=\frac{1}{9}q_1q_2$ but incorrectly ignores the inverse-square dependence on distance.}
  \Correct{It decreases by a factor of $36$.}{By Coulomb's law, $F=k\frac{|q_1q_2|}{r^2}$. The charge product scales by $(1/3)(1/3)=1/9$ and the squared distance scales by $(2r)^2=4r^2$, so $F' = F\cdot \frac{1/9}{4}=\frac{F}{36}$; the force decreases by a factor of $36$.}
  \Wrong{It increases by a factor of $36$.}{This reverses the scaling in Coulomb's law. Reducing charge magnitudes and increasing separation both decrease the force rather than increase it.}
  \Wrong{It decreases by a factor of $4$.}{This includes the distance effect ($1/(2^2)=1/4$) but ignores that both charges were also reduced, which introduces an additional $1/9$ factor.}
}

\QBlock{Q10}{Escape speed from a spherical world is $v_e=\sqrt{\frac{2GM}{R}}$. World Y has mass $M$ and radius $R$. World X has mass $0.40M$ and radius $0.20R$. Which statement is correct?}{
  \Wrong{World X has a lower escape speed than World Y because it has smaller mass.}{This considers only $M$ and ignores the dependence on $R$. Escape speed depends on the ratio $M/R$, not on $M$ alone.}
  \Wrong{World X has the same escape speed as World Y because both $M$ and $R$ are smaller.}{Having both parameters smaller does not guarantee cancellation. You must compare $\frac{M}{R}$ for each world.}
  \Correct{World X has a higher escape speed than World Y (by a factor of about $1.41$).}{From $v_e\propto\sqrt{M/R}$, the ratio is $\frac{v_{e,X}}{v_{e,Y}}=\sqrt{\frac{0.40M/0.20R}{M/R}}=\sqrt{2}\approx 1.41$. Even though X is less massive, it is much more compact (smaller $R$), which increases $M/R$ and thus increases escape speed.}
  \Wrong{The escape speed depends on the spacecraft's mass, so it cannot be compared without that information.}{In Newtonian gravity (neglecting drag), $v_e=\sqrt{2GM/R}$ is independent of the escaping object's mass. The spacecraft mass cancels when deriving the expression from energy conservation.}
}

\QBlock{Q11}{A constant force $\vec F=(-2.5\ \mathrm{N})\,\hat\imath+(6.0\ \mathrm{N})\,\hat\jmath$ acts on a particle as it moves in the plane from $\vec r_i=(-1.2\,\hat\imath+0.5\,\hat\jmath)\ \mathrm{m}$ to $\vec r_f=(3.8\,\hat\imath+4.5\,\hat\jmath)\ \mathrm{m}$. What is the work done by the force?}{
  \Correct{$11.5\ \mathrm{J}$}{Work is the dot product $W=\vec F\cdot\Delta\vec r=F_x\Delta x+F_y\Delta y$. Here $\Delta\vec r=(3.8-(-1.2))\hat\imath+(4.5-0.5)\hat\jmath=5.0\hat\imath+4.0\hat\jmath$, so $W=(-2.5)(5.0)+(6.0)(4.0)=-12.5+24=11.5\ \mathrm{J}$.}
  \Wrong{$41.6\ \mathrm{J}$}{This comes from incorrectly using $W\approx |\vec F|\,|\Delta\vec r|$, which only works when force is parallel to displacement. Here the force is not parallel to the displacement.}
  \Wrong{$24.0\ \mathrm{J}$}{This uses only the $y$-component contribution $F_y\Delta y$ and ignores the negative work from the $x$-component. Work adds contributions from all components via the dot product.}
  \Wrong{$-12.5\ \mathrm{J}$}{This uses only $F_x\Delta x$ and ignores the positive work from the $y$-component. The total work is the sum $F_x\Delta x+F_y\Delta y$.}
}

\QBlock{Q12}{A $3.2\ \mathrm{kg}$ cart moving to the right at $4.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$ hits a bumper and rebounds to the left at $2.0\ \mathrm{m/s}$. The contact time is $0.15\ \mathrm{s}$. What is the magnitude of the average force on the cart during the collision?}{
  \Wrong{$96\ \mathrm{N}$}{This can result from using $\Delta v=4.5-2.0=2.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$ (forgetting the velocity reverses sign). The correct $\Delta v$ must use $v_f=-2.0\ \mathrm{m/s}$.}
  \Correct{$139\ \mathrm{N}$}{Use impulse-momentum: $F_{\text{avg}}=\frac{\Delta p}{\Delta t}=\frac{m(v_f-v_i)}{\Delta t}$. Taking right as positive, $v_i=+4.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$ and $v_f=-2.0\ \mathrm{m/s}$, so $\Delta v=-6.5\ \mathrm{m/s}$ and $|F_{\text{avg}}|=\left|\frac{3.2(-6.5)}{0.15}\right|\approx 1.39\times 10^2\ \mathrm{N}$.}
  \Wrong{$31\ \mathrm{N}$}{This is too small and typically comes from dividing by the wrong time interval or using only one speed instead of the change in velocity. Impulse depends on $v_f-v_i$.}
  \Wrong{None of the above.}{Option B matches the impulse calculation (to rounding). Therefore it is not correct that none of the above is correct.}
}

\QBlock{Q13}{A $72\ \mathrm{kg}$ snowboarder is initially at rest on nearly frictionless snow. A friend tosses a $250\ \mathrm{g}$ snowball directly toward the snowboarder at $90\ \mathrm{km/h}$, and the snowboarder catches it (the snowball sticks). What is the snowboarder's speed immediately after the catch?}{
  \Wrong{$0.31\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{This typically comes from forgetting to convert $90\ \mathrm{km/h}$ to $\mathrm{m/s}$ (treating $90$ as $90\ \mathrm{m/s}$). Unit conversion is essential before applying momentum conservation.}
  \Wrong{$25\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{This incorrectly assumes the snowboarder ends up moving at (or near) the snowball's initial speed. In a perfectly inelastic catch with a much larger combined mass, the final speed must be much smaller.}
  \Correct{$0.087\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{Treat the catch as perfectly inelastic and conserve momentum: $m_b v_b=(m_s+m_b)v_f$. Convert $90\ \mathrm{km/h}$ to $v_b=25\ \mathrm{m/s}$ and use $m_b=0.250\ \mathrm{kg}$, $m_s=72\ \mathrm{kg}$: $v_f=\frac{0.250\cdot 25}{72.25}\approx 0.0865\ \mathrm{m/s}\approx 0.087\ \mathrm{m/s}$.}
  \Wrong{$0.0087\ \mathrm{m/s}$}{This is a factor-of-10 error, often from a mistaken km/h conversion (e.g., dividing by $3.6$ twice) or misplacing a decimal in the final division.}
}

